of Thermal Energy Systems)\n\nDownload keyboard_arrow_down\n\nDownload PDF\nDownload PDF with Cover\nDownload XML\nDownload Epub\n\nBrowse Figures\n\nVersions Notes\n\n## Abstract\n\nParameter estimation of electrical vehicle batteries in the presence of temperature effect is addressed in this work. A simple parametric temperature dependent battery model is used for this purpose where the temperature dependence is described by static relationships. In the first stage, battery core temperature is estimated and, afterwards, SOC and capacity are estimated by a sliding model observer.\n\nDue to the nonlinear nature of parametric lithium-ion battery models and the fact that parameters might also depend on time and/or external variables, the computational complexity of battery parameter estimation can be demanding. Wang et al. [14] overcame this problem by a parallel Java algorithm implemented on GPU (CUDA) architecture. The authors of [15] developed and compared three different solutions for the internal resistance estimation of lithium-ion batteries (direct resistance estimation, Extended Kalman Filter (EKF), and recursive least squares) and concluded that EKF approach performed the best in terms of computational efficiency.\n\nIn our previous work [16], we proposed a parameter estimation for lithium ion batteries based on their first-order equivalent circuit model. The aim of the present paper is to generalize that work to the case when the temperature dependence of the parameters are also taken into account in such a way that a computationally effective way of temperature dependent parameters important from the viewpoint of applicability (e.g., actual capacity) could be given for implementation in future BMSs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMTEC-SOC: A Multi-Physics Aging-Aware Model for Smartphone Battery SOC Estimation Under Diverse User Behaviors (https://www.mdpi.com/2313-0105/12/4/130)\nciteturn14search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; This study proposes a multi-physics coupled SOC estimation framework, termed the Multi-Physics Thermo-Electrochemical Coupled SOC Model (MTEC-SOC), to characterize battery behavior under representative user-load conditions within controlled ambient thermal boundaries. ... Similarly, the ohmic resistance {R}_{0} is modeled as a temperature-dependent effective parameter within the calibrated range.\n\nTo more accurately capture the transient voltage response under diverse user behaviors, we discarded the fixed-value approximation for polarization voltage and introduced a dynamic polarization model based on a first-order RC network. The dynamic polarization voltage {V}_{p}\\left(t\\right) is continuously updated using the following discrete-time state equation:\n\n{V}_{p}\\left(t\\right)={V}_{p}(t-\\Delta t){e}^{{\\displaystyle \\frac{-\\Delta t}{\\tau \\left(T\\right)}}}+I\\left(t\\right){R}_{p}\\left(T\\right)(1-{e}^{{\\displaystyle \\frac{-\\Delta t}{\\tau \\left(T\\right)}}})\n\n{R}_{p}\\left(T\\right)={R}_{p,ref}[1+{\\alpha }_{p}(T-{T}_{ref})]\n\n\\tau \\left(T\\right)={R}_{p}\\left(T\\right){C}_{p}\n\nHere, {R}_{p}\\left(T\\right) is treated as an effective temperature-sensitive polarization resistance calibrated from the EIS datasets measured at 15, 25, and 35 °C. As the battery temperature evolves, the polarization branch is updated in real time through {R}_{p}\\left(T\\right) and the corresponding time constant tau\\left(T\\right), so that temperature affects not only the thermal state itself but also the transient electrical kinetics represented by the RC network. Similarly, the ohmic resistance {R}_{0} is modeled as a temperature-dependent effective parameter within the calibrated range. Where \\Delta t is the sampling time step, \\tau ={R}_{p}·{C}_{p} represents the polarization time constant, and {R}_{p} and {C}_{p} are the equivalent polarization resistance and capacitance, respectively.\n\n--------